    strengthAxis: "detail",
    approximateScore: 90,
    excerpt: `The word for "pain" in Portuguese has seventeen inflections that English compresses into one, and in Room 412 at Boston Medical Center, that compression almost killed a woman. Mrs. Oliveira had been describing her chest pain as "uma dor que aperta" — a pain that squeezes — for three hours. The intake nurse had written "chest pain" and moved on. In English, pain is pain. In Portuguese, a pain that squeezes is different from a pain that stabs, which is different from a pain that burns. The distinction matters because a squeezing pain in the chest is a classic sign of cardiac distress, not just discomfort. When I arrived as a volunteer interpreter and translated the full phrase, the nurse's face changed. Mrs. Oliveira was in the cardiac unit within twenty minutes.

I am not a hero in this story. I am a seventeen-year-old who speaks Portuguese because my grandmother raised me and who happened to be in the right hallway at the right time. But the fact that "happened to be" is what it took — that a major hospital didn't have a Portuguese interpreter available for a Portuguese-speaking patient having a cardiac event — is the systemic failure I can't stop thinking about.

I now volunteer twelve hours per week in the interpreter services department. I have learned that language access in American hospitals is a patchwork — some facilities have professional interpreters, some use phone lines, some rely on family members or, worst of all, Google Translate. Mrs. Oliveira's seventeen inflections of pain deserved a human listener, not an algorithm. The difference between "pain" and "a pain that squeezes" is not a linguistic curiosity. It is, sometimes, a life.`,
