    excerpt: `Rust is not decay. Rust is iron telling a story about where it has been, and I have spent six months reading a 1972 Schwinn Paramount like a novel. I found it in my neighbor's garage, leaning against a lawnmower, its chrome lugs the color of dried blood. The tires were cracked. The chain was frozen. The downtube had a dent that suggested it had once been dropped from a significant height by someone who didn't love it enough.

I bought it for $75. The restoration cost $200 in parts, forty hours of labor, and one tetanus scare that my mother still references when she wants to win an argument. But the real cost was the question the rust asked me: why does this steel corrode at different rates in different places? The chrome-plated lugs had thin, surface-level oxidation. The raw steel seat stays had deep pitting. The aluminum seatpost had white powdery corrosion that looked nothing like the iron oxide on the frame. Three metals, three kinds of decay, three stories about the relationship between a material and its environment.

I started researching corrosion chemistry. I learned that rust is iron oxide — Fe₂O₃ — and that the rate of its formation depends on moisture, salt, acidity, and the presence of dissimilar metals in contact (galvanic corrosion, which is why the aluminum seatpost corroded differently where it met the steel frame). Each fact made the bicycle more interesting, not less. By the time I finished the restoration, I understood the frame better than I understood most of my textbooks — because I had touched every inch of it, diagnosed every failure, and rebuilt it with the kind of knowledge that only comes from fixing something with your hands.`,
    counselorNote: "The three-metals observation is genuine materials science discovered through hands-on work. The tetanus scare is a humanizing detail. The galvanic corrosion explanation — aluminum against steel — shows the applicant has done real research. Lehigh's engineering program will appreciate the learn-by-fixing approach."
